import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Is 3 years old for a car battery in Dubai?",
    a: "For a conventional flooded battery, yes — three years is around the typical end of life in the UAE. Many will go longer with covered parking and regular long drives, but it's a sensible point to test the battery before each summer.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do car batteries die faster in summer or winter in the UAE?",
    a: "Heat does the damage in summer, but many batteries actually fail in the cooler months or at the first hot spell after them. The summer degrades the battery; a cooler morning or an unusually demanding start is what finally exposes it.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does an AGM battery last longer in the heat?",
    a: "Generally, yes. AGM batteries are sealed and lose less water, which is one of the main heat-related failure modes. They cost more, and they're required on many stop-start and premium cars anyway.",
  },
];
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="how-long-car-battery-lasts-uae"
  service="battery_testing"
  lead="Batteries that last four to five years in cooler countries often manage only two or three in the UAE. Here's why the heat is so hard on them — and what actually helps."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> about 2–3 years for a typical conventional battery in Dubai, often less for cars
      parked outdoors in the sun; AGM batteries typically last longer. Rated life on the label is the ceiling, not the
      average.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why heat shortens battery life</h2>
  <p>
    A lead-acid car battery works through chemical reactions, and like most chemical reactions they speed up with
    temperature. That includes the unwanted ones. Heat accelerates corrosion of the internal lead grids, causes water in
    the electrolyte to evaporate, and speeds up self-discharge when the car is parked.
  </p>
  <p>
    Under the bonnet of a car parked in a Dubai summer, temperatures are far higher than the air temperature. The battery
    sits there, day after day, being slowly cooked. The damage is cumulative and permanent — which is why a battery can
    seem fine in August and then fail on a cool morning in November.
  </p>

  <h2>Typical battery life in the UAE by type</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Battery type</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical life in the UAE</th>
          <th scope="col">Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Conventional flooded</strong></td>
          <td>About 2–3 years</td>
          <td>Most affected by water loss in heat</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>EFB</strong></td>
          <td>About 2–4 years</td>
          <td>Tougher than conventional, built for stop-start</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>AGM</strong></td>
          <td>About 3–5 years</td>
          <td>Sealed; generally the most heat-tolerant</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>These are typical ranges, not guarantees — parking, driving pattern and the car's electrical load all matter.</p>

  <h2>What else wears a battery out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Short trips.</strong> Starting the engine takes a big gulp of energy. A 10-minute drive doesn't fully put it
      back, so a car used only for short hops slowly runs its battery down.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Long periods parked.</strong> Modern cars draw a small current even when off. Leave one for two or three
      weeks — common over the summer travel season — and the battery can go flat, and deep discharges damage it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Heavy electrical load.</strong> Dash cams on parking mode, aftermarket lights, amplifiers and fridges all add
      load.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The wrong battery.</strong> A conventional battery fitted to a car that needs EFB or AGM will fail early,
      often within months.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A faulty alternator.</strong> Undercharging or overcharging both shorten battery life.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to make a battery last longer in Dubai</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Park in the shade or underground whenever you can.</li>
    <li>Take a longer drive (30 minutes or more) at least once a week.</li>
    <li>Before travelling for more than two weeks, ask about disconnecting the battery or using a smart maintainer.</li>
    <li>Keep terminals clean and tight.</li>
    <li>Have the battery tested before summer once it's more than two years old — our <a href="/battery-testing-dubai/">battery test</a> is free.</li>
    <li>When you replace it, fit the correct type — see <a href="/guides/agm-vs-efb-vs-conventional-battery/">AGM vs EFB vs conventional</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    More practical tips in <a href="/guides/car-battery-care-dubai-summer/">car battery care in a Dubai summer</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
